# Slide Deck Outline — Set Up Your AI Workflow

> **Source of truth:** `ai-workflow-for-ux-designers-lesson.md`
> All content changes (activities, concepts, examples) must be made there first, then reflected here.
> This file contains only slide-specific concerns: layout types, visual hints, kickers, and structure.
> **Copy standard:** every Kicker, Title, Lead, and bullet on this deck should be readable in one glance. No sentence over ~15 words on a slide. If a point needs a qualifier or an example, it goes in speaker notes or the lesson file — not on the slide.

> **Visual system cross-referenced from the real Design Thinking deck.** The actual built deck (`1. Design Thinking for UX Designer.pdf`, 36 slides, built in Pitch) diverges from its own outline file in a few ways worth knowing before you build this one:
> - Activity slides on screen show **no timer** — just an orange-outlined "ACTIVITY" pill and a huge bold question, full stop. Timing lives in the facilitator's head/speaker notes, not on the slide.
> - Each numbered assignment gets its **own full slide** (Assignment 1, 2, 3 each standalone) — never a combined two-column "Assignment 1 + 2" slide.
> - There is **no dedicated closing/quote slide** — the real deck ends right after the last assignment, then a divider ("What to Do Before Next Session"), then the assignments. This outline keeps a closing slide as a deliberate small addition — drop it if you want to match the reference deck exactly.
> This file has been corrected below to follow the real deck's actual patterns, not the assumptions in the previous draft.

> **How to use this file**
> Paste both this file and `ai-workflow-for-ux-designers-lesson.md` into your AI tool with the instruction below to generate a new slide deck.

---

## Prompt to use

```
Using the outline in this file, create an HTML slide deck for Set Up Your AI Workflow,
matching the visual system of the Design Thinking deck (built in Pitch) exactly.

Save the file to Library/lessons/ai-workflow-for-ux-designers/materials/deck.html.

VISUAL SYSTEM — cross-referenced from the real deck, apply exactly:

Colour roles (use these, not a generic token palette):
- Marigold/gold (~#F5C244) — full-bleed background for every stage/section divider. Nothing
  else on these slides except a small dark kicker label and one huge bold dark headline.
- Ink navy (~#1A1D2E) — reserved for the rare "big definition" statement slide (max 1–2 in
  the whole deck). Gold headline, white body text, one phrase underlined in a soft violet.
- White — the default background for almost everything else: activities, comparisons,
  stat slides, grids, quotes.
- Iris/purple (~#6C5CE7) — the ONE strong accent colour. Used for: the "you are here"
  highlighted card in any pipeline/process row, numbered circle badges, bullet dots on
  light cards, the filled column in a two-column comparison, organic decorative blob shapes.
- Soft pastel tints, one per content section, rotating: blue, lavender, mint/green, peach,
  pink/rose. Each "grid of cards" slide (tool categories, options, stage activities) picks
  ONE pastel tint for all its cards — do not mix tints within a single grid.
- Amber/orange OUTLINE pill (not filled) — the tag component for "ACTIVITY" and
  "ASSIGNMENT N" labels. Rounded pill, coloured border and text, transparent/white fill.
- Pale mint-green card — the AI-prompt callout component. Bold italic header "🤖 AI in
  Practice — Paste this prompt into AI tools:" followed by the prompt itself in italic,
  regular weight. Reuse this exact component every time a slide includes a runnable prompt.

Layout rules:
- Section/stage dividers: solid gold, full-bleed. Small kicker ("Stage 1" style) + one huge
  bold headline. Nothing else — no icons, no supporting text, no numeral treatment.
- Activity slides: white background, orange-outline "ACTIVITY" pill top-left, one huge bold
  question or instruction below it. No visible timer, no diagram, maximum whitespace.
- Assignment slides: white background, orange-outline "ASSIGNMENT N" pill top-left, bold
  headline, a purple-bullet-dot list below (nested sub-bullets allowed), and the mint-green
  AI-prompt card at the bottom of the SAME slide if there's a relevant prompt.
- Grid-of-cards slides (options, categories, stage activities): rounded cards in one pastel
  tint, each with a simple flat two-tone icon illustration, a bold card title, and one short
  grey description line. 2–4 columns depending on card count.
- Pipeline/process rows (the 5-stage cycle, CARE, etc.): equal-width bordered cards, small
  number top-left, bold label, one short grey line at the bottom. The "current" or
  "highlighted" card in any row is solid iris/purple with white text — every other card
  stays white/light-grey with a thin border.
- Two-column comparisons: left column plain light-grey card, right column solid iris/purple
  card with an amber/gold all-caps heading label and white bullet list.
- Real photos are welcome for humour or human-context slides (the opening activity, the
  cover) — don't force everything into abstract iconography.
- Prefer huge amounts of whitespace over dense slides. When in doubt, cut, don't shrink.
```

