## Slide structure

> **33 slides.**
> Arc: Hook → Mindset → Technical Literacy (incl. Markdown+HTML pipeline) → Toolkit (CARE + feed-forward + AI-assisted/generated) → Four Designer Buckets (synthesis, process map, prototype, other — incl. artifact chain) → Hands-on Build → Close.
> Maps 1:1 onto the lesson file's 5 phases: Sections 2–3 = Phase 1, Section 4 = Phase 2, Section 5 = Phase 3, Section 6 = Phase 4, Section 7 = Phase 5.

---

## 1. Introduction

---

### COVER
- Year: 2026
- Day label: Session 2
- Title: Set Up\nYour *AI Workflow*
- Subtitle: A deliberate way to bring AI into the design process
- Author: Winnie Nguyen
- 🎨 Visual hint: Matches the Design Thinking cover exactly — dark gradient background (green-to-black), big bold white title on the left, presenter name + short credentials + circular avatar photo bottom-left, full-bleed real photo on the right panel (designer's hands at a laptop, chat interface + Figma file visible). Warm and human, not futuristic.

---

### ACTIVITY — Opening Activity
- Pill: ACTIVITY
- Title: One time AI helped. One time it quietly hurt you.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background. Orange-outline "ACTIVITY" pill top-left, like the reference deck's opening activity slide. One huge bold black question, nothing else — no diagram, no timer shown on-slide. (Facilitator note, not on slide: 2 minutes.)

---

## 2. Why Now

---

### STATEMENT — Everyone's already using AI
- Kicker: The numbers
- Title: 91% of designers\nused AI in 2026.\n*7 tools, on average.*
- Lead: The question isn't whether you use AI. It's whether you use it deliberately.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background. Two large stat numbers side by side in bold display type — "91%" and "7" — with small grey captions underneath each. No icons needed; let the numbers carry it, matching the reference deck's plain, type-led statement slides.

---

### STATEMENT — But results are split
- Kicker: Same tools, different outcomes
- Title: 36% said design\ngot better. 35% said\n*worse.*
- Lead: Same tools. Different results. The difference is the workflow behind them.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Horizontal stacked bar — three segments in grey/black/iris-purple tones for Better / Worse / Same, not the multi-colour scheme used before. Small caption below: "Figma, State of the Designer 2026."

---

### STATEMENT — The role is shifting
- Kicker: What's changing
- Title: From *maker*\nto strategist\nand editor.
- Lead: AI is a set of gloves, not a new hand. Stay the strategist.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background, simple before/after icon pair in the reference deck's flat two-tone icon style — hand + pencil ("Maker") on the left, hand + steering wheel ("Strategist / Editor") on the right, thin arrow between. Icons in a single accent tint, not full colour illustration.

---

## 3. The AI Mindset

---

### STATEMENT — Augment, don't replace
- Kicker: Mindset 1
- Title: AI takes the\ntedious work.\n*You keep the judgment.*
- Lead: AI handles what's repetitive. Authorship, taste, and ethics stay with you.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Solid gold background, matching the reference deck's rare full-colour statement slide. Bold dark headline, grey/dark body line below. No icon — let the colour and type do the work.

---

### STATEMENT — AI as intern, not oracle
- Kicker: Mindset 2
- Title: Treat AI like\na talented intern.\n*Not an oracle.*
- Lead: Fast, tireless, occasionally brilliant — and it still needs supervision.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background. Simple two-tone icon: a person at a desk with a small assistant icon beside them, both facing the same document — same flat illustration style as the reference deck's activity-card icons. Small caption bottom corner: "Meet Ari."

---

### STATEMENT — This is a hype cycle
- Kicker: Mindset 3
- Title: We've been here\nbefore. Remember VR?
- Lead: Over-promise, then a dip, then durable use. Expect the dip.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background, simple thin-line hype-cycle curve (peak then dip then plateau), two small labels on the curve. Matches the reference deck's plain line-diagram style (like the tangled-string A-to-B diagram) — one clean line, minimal colour, mostly whitespace.

---

### ACTIVITY — Neighbour Discussion
- Pill: ACTIVITY
- Title: Share your "helped" and "hurt" moments. What was different?
- 🎨 Visual hint: Same treatment as the opening Activity slide — white background, orange-outline "ACTIVITY" pill, one bold question, no timer shown, no diagram.

---

## 4. Technical Literacy Primer

---

### SECTION — Stage divider
- Kicker: Before you touch a tool
- Title: A few words\nyou'll keep hearing
- 🎨 Visual hint: Solid gold, full-bleed — matches the reference deck's stage dividers exactly (e.g. "EMPATHISE," "DEFINE"). Small kicker label, one huge bold dark headline, nothing else. No numeral, no supporting subtitle.

---

### STATEMENT — What is a .md file?
- Kicker: Word 1 of 3
- Title: Markdown is just\nplain text\n*with typing rules.*
- Lead: `**bold**` instead of a button, `## Heading` instead of a menu. It's what AI tools read and write by default.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background. Split card: left shows raw Markdown (`## Heading`, `**bold**`) in monospace, right shows the same content rendered as a formatted doc. Simple arrow between labelled "renders as" — flat, minimal, matches the reference deck's plain comparison cards.

---

### STATEMENT — What is HTML?
- Kicker: Word 2 of 3
- Title: HTML is what\na browser actually\n*draws on screen.*
- Lead: `<button>` is a button, `<img>` is an image. An AI-generated prototype is just an HTML file.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background. Code snippet `<nav><button>Home</button></nav>` next to a small rendered nav bar with a button — direct code-to-visual correspondence, plain and minimal.

---

### DIAGRAM — Markdown in, HTML out
- Kicker: How they work together
- Title: Raw insight in.\n*Formatted report out.*
- Lead: Synthesis themes — Markdown. The report you show stakeholders — HTML.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Three connected boxes in a horizontal flow, white background — reuse the exact three-step visual from the AI Prototype Development deck's "What is an AI-assisted prototype?" slide, relabelled for research instead of prototyping: box 1 shows a Markdown-style synthesis doc ("Interview themes, `## Heading` + bullets — you write") → arrow → an "AI" box (solid iris/purple) → arrow → box 3 shows a styled browser-window report with theme cards and a small chart ("Research report — runs in browser"). Same three-step flow, same component, so the pattern is recognisable when it resurfaces as "prototype pattern" in that later session.

---

### DIAGRAM — HTML nesting = Atomic Design nesting
- Kicker: A parallel you already know
- Title: You've already\nlearned this structure.
- Lead: A `<nav>` holding `<button>` tags is an Organism holding Atoms. Same idea, new name.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Two parallel nested-box diagrams side by side, white background. Left, "ATOMIC DESIGN": Organism → Molecule → Atom. Right, "HTML": `<nav>` → `<div>` → `<button>`. Matching sizes/colours across both so the parallel is obvious at a glance.

---

### NUMBERED — Local folder setup: pick your organisation
- Kicker: Word 3 of 3
- Title: One project,\none folder.\n*Organised your way.*
- Lead: Start on day one, not the day you open an AI coding tool.
- 3 options (cards, one pastel tint — e.g. soft blue — across all three, matching the reference deck's activity-grid style):
  - **By stage** — `01-empathise/ … 05-test/`. Mirrors your process.
  - **By file type** — `notes/ assets/ prompts/ exports/`. Grab one kind fast.
  - **By deliverable** — `research/ design-system/ prototype/ presentation/`. Output over history.
- Footer note: *No wrong choice. Only mistake: no structure.*
- 🎨 Visual hint: Three rounded cards, one shared pastel tint, each with a simple flat folder-tree icon in the tint's accent colour — same visual family as the reference deck's "Discovery Activities" card grid.

---

## 5. The AI Workflow Toolkit

---

### SECTION — Stage divider
- Kicker: How to ask well
- Title: Build your\ntoolkit
- 🎨 Visual hint: Solid gold, full-bleed, matching every other stage divider in this deck. Kicker + headline only.

---

### STATEMENT — AI-assisted vs. AI-generated
- Kicker: The throughline
- Title: AI-generated:\nAI decides.\n*AI-assisted: you decide.*
- Lead: AI-generated, AI decides. AI-assisted, you decide and AI assembles. Default to AI-assisted.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Two-column comparison, matching the reference deck's "Why design fails without a process" slide exactly — left column plain light-grey card ("AI-GENERATED," muted), right column solid iris/purple card ("AI-ASSISTED," amber/gold heading label, white bullet list).

---

### PROCESS — The CARE framework
- Kicker: How to ask well
- Title: CARE:\nContext · Ask ·\n*Rules · Examples.*
- 4 parts (numbered cards):
  1. **Context** — the situation
  2. **Ask** — the specific request
  3. **Rules** — constraints, tone, format
  4. **Examples** — show, don't tell
- 🎨 Visual hint: Four equal-width bordered cards in a row, small number top-left, bold letter (C · A · R · E), one-word definition below — same treatment as the reference deck's "5-Stage Cycle" cards, 4 cards instead of 5, white/light-grey fill, no highlight needed.

---

### STATEMENT — Feed forward, don't start fresh
- Kicker: A Context habit
- Title: Reuse what you\nalready have.\n*Don't retype it.*
- Lead: A new chat starts at zero. Your folder doesn't. Check it before you retype anything.
- 🎨 Visual hint: White background, before/after pair — left muted ("STARTING FRESH": chat bubble, long retyped block, small clock icon), right in iris/purple ("FEED FORWARD": folder icon, arrow straight into a chat bubble). Simple two-tone icons, matching the reference deck's flat illustration style.

---

### STATEMENT — Vague prompt vs. CARE prompt
- Kicker: See the difference
- Title: "Make this\nbetter" vs.\n*a real brief.*
- 2-column comparison:
  - **Vague** (plain light-grey card) — No context. No constraints. No example.
  - **CARE-structured** (solid iris/purple card) — Context, Ask, Rules, Examples — filled in.
- Lead: Live demo — same task, both prompts, compare the output.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Same two-column comparison component as the AI-assisted/AI-generated slide — plain card left, solid purple card right with C/A/R/E labels visible in white text.

---

### NUMBERED — AI tool categories
- Kicker: What each is for
- Title: Chat. Coding.\n*In-tool.*
- 3 categories (one shared pastel tint, e.g. lavender):
  - **AI chat tools** — research, ideation, writing
  - **AI coding tools** — pattern into working code
  - **In-tool AI features** — renaming, detection, suggestions
- Footer note: *Depth beats breadth.*
- 🎨 Visual hint: Three rounded cards, one pastel tint, each with a simple flat icon (speech bubble / code brackets / design-tool icon) and one short line — matching the reference deck's "Synthesis Activities" card style.

---

## 6. Where AI Fits in Your Design Process

---

### SECTION — Stage divider
- Kicker: Four things you do
- Title: Where AI fits\nin your process
- 🎨 Visual hint: Solid gold, full-bleed, matching every other divider. Kicker + headline only.

---

### NUMBERED — The four buckets
- Kicker: Not a new framework
- Title: Everything you do\n*sorts into four buckets.*
- 4 cards (grid, one pastel tint for all):
  1. **Synthesising Research**
  2. **Setting Up Your Process Map**
  3. **Prototyping**
  4. **Other Activities**
- Lead: Same design process you already know — just named by what you're actually doing.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Reuse the reference deck's "grid of cards" pattern (like tool categories) — 4 cards, single pastel tint, flat 2-tone icon per card (magnifying glass / map / screen stack / toolbox). No stage names on this slide — buckets only.

---

### STATEMENT — AI's usefulness is uneven
- Kicker: Not evenly spread
- Title: Strongest at Synthesis\n+ Prototyping.\n*Riskiest at the judgment calls.*
- Lead: The more a task is "decide," the more it stays you.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Same 4-card row from the previous slide, now Synthesising Research and Prototyping cards solid iris/purple with white text (strongest), Process Map and Other Activities outline-only with a thin warning-toned border (judgment-heavy) — same "highlight the current one" logic as the reference deck's "pattern most teams follow" slide.

---

### NUMBERED — Bucket by bucket: what stays yours
- Kicker: The full map
- Title: What AI does well.\n*What stays yours.*
- 4 rows (compact grid, one per bucket):
  - **Synthesising Research** — AI: clusters themes. Yours: which theme is load-bearing.
  - **Process Map** — AI: drafts a first-pass map. Yours: sequencing and priority.
  - **Prototyping** — AI: assembles from a pattern. Yours: spacing and hierarchy.
  - **Other Activities** — AI: fast first drafts. Yours: what's true, what ships.
- 🎨 Visual hint: 4-row table, white background, bucket name as a small iris/purple pill per row, "AI does well" / "Stays yours" as two plain text columns — same compact reference style as the reference deck's plain text-and-icon rows.

---

### DIAGRAM — Each bucket hands forward an artifact
- Kicker: The chain is the workflow
- Title: One artifact per bucket.\n*Fed forward, not retyped.*
- 4 artifacts, left to right:
  1. Synthesising Research → **research synthesis doc**
  2. Process Map → **journey map / sitemap / task flow**
  3. Prototyping → **prototype pattern**
  4. Other Activities → **test synthesis / findings doc**
- Lead: Each artifact becomes your next Context. Never re-enter a task from zero.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Same 4-card pipeline row, each card showing a small flat document icon + artifact name, thin arrows labelled "→ becomes Context for." Same bordered-card style as the bucket-intro slide — no new visual language.

---

### NUMBERED — Quick reference: buckets → Design Thinking stages
- Kicker: Still the same 5 stages
- Title: This maps onto\n*what you already know.*
- 4 rows (compact grid):
  - **Synthesising Research** → Empathise (some Test)
  - **Process Map** → Define → Ideate
  - **Prototyping** → Prototype
  - **Other Activities** → Cross-cutting
- 🎨 Visual hint: Small compact table, plain text, no heavy styling — a bridge slide reassuring students this is the same 5-stage cycle from Design Thinking, just organised by task instead of by stage.

---

### ACTIVITY — Activity 1: Bucket Mapping + Artifact Canvas
- Pill: ACTIVITY
- Title: Draft your AI + artifact map
- Lead: Use your real project. One bucket at a time.
- 4-column grid: SYNTHESISING RESEARCH · PROCESS MAP · PROTOTYPING · OTHER ACTIVITIES — solid iris/purple pill header per column, 3 blank rows underneath each (AI helps with / stays yours / artifact to save)
- AI prompt card: *"I'm a UX designer working on [project]. My users are [who they are]. For each bucket — synthesising research, process mapping, prototyping, other activities — suggest: 1 thing AI can help with, 1 thing that stays my judgment, and 1 artifact I should save to carry forward."*
- 🎨 Visual hint: Direct match to the reference deck's "Draft your Design Process" slide — orange-outline "ACTIVITY" pill top-left, bold title, 4 solid-purple pill headers in a row, blank light-grey pill rows underneath each, pale mint-green AI-prompt card at the bottom (bold italic "🤖 AI in Practice" header, prompt in italic below). One slide does the whole activity, exactly like the reference.

